---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="CLI reference"
   summary="Complete command reference for the a11ied CLI. Install with npm and run commands using the <code>a1</code> shorthand (or <code>a11ied</code>)."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section class="ledger">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Command map</p>
         <h2>Command families</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag</code><span>Look up criteria, levels, search results, and coverage strategy.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>inspect</code><span>Check which criteria apply to a page.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr</code><span>Control a screen reader session with low-level actions.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>axe</code><span>Run axe-core scans against a URL.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>doctor</code><span>Report runtime details, browser policy, target availability, and recording diagnostics.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>mcp</code><span>Start the stdio MCP server.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>help-all</code><span>Print the entire command tree in one shot — useful for agents discovering the CLI.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Subcommands</p>
         <h2>Subcommands by family</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="mini-grid">
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>wcag</h3>
            <p><code>levels</code>, <code>criteria</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>coverage</code></p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>inspect</h3>
            <p><code>applicable</code>, <code>criterion</code></p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>sr</h3>
            <p><code>start</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>stop</code>, plus <code>focus</code>, <code>next</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>press</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>do</code></p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>axe</h3>
            <p>Top-level command. Options: <code>--url</code>, <code>--criterion</code>, <code>--level</code>, <code>--rule</code></p>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Example</p>
            <h2>One criterion, end to end</h2>
         </div>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag show 4.1.3 --json
a1 inspect criterion 4.1.3 --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json
a1 axe --criterion 4.1.3 --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         This sequence runs through three steps: lookup, applicability check, and evidence gathering.
         Pattern execution and verification are available through the programmatic API —
         see <a href="/api-reference">API reference</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Most commands accept <code>--target</code>. If you omit it, a11ied chooses VoiceOver on
         macOS, NVDA on Windows, or the virtual target elsewhere. Use
         <code>--target virtual</code> with <code>--allow-virtual</code> when you want the simulated
         screen reader. For manual runs, stick with the default real target.
      </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">see also</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            For guided walkthroughs, see <a href="/workflows">workflows</a>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Browser policy</p>
         <h2>How browser-backed commands pick Chromium</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Browser-backed commands such as <code>axe</code> and the focus or dialog probes look
         for an existing local Chromium-family browser first.
         a11ied prefers Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium if one is already on the machine.
      </p>
      <p>
         If none of those are available, a11ied falls back to Playwright's managed Chromium
         when it is already installed. If no usable browser is present, the command tells you to run
         <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor
a1 doctor --json
npx playwright install chromium</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Agent support</p>
         <h2>Print the full command tree</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 help-all</code></pre>
      <p>
         Outputs every subcommand and flag in one pass. Useful for AI agents exploring the CLI
         surface programmatically.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Tip</p>
         <h2>Start with the smallest command</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         If you only need criterion text, use <code>wcag show</code>. No browser is required. The CLI
         is layered so you can pick the right level of effort for each task.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
